# Utils Module Rules

## Purpose
Helper functions, database utility functions, and specialized utility modules. These are higher-level utilities that may use lib functions.

## Entry Points
- `src/utils/cn.ts` - ClassName utility (if exists)
- `src/utils/db/**` - Database utility functions
- `src/utils/hook/**` - Custom React hooks
- `src/utils/db/registerPayment.ts` - Payment registration utility
- `src/utils/db/template/template.ts` - Template utility
- `src/utils/db/userLogs.ts` - User logging utility

## Allowed Imports
- `@/lib/*` - Core utilities (db, auth, utils)
- `@/components/*` - Components (for hooks)
- React and React DOM (for hooks)
- Drizzle ORM for database utilities
- External utility libraries

## Forbidden Imports
- `@/app/**` - No app route imports
- Direct database clients (use `@/lib/db`)

## Coding Conventions

### Database Utilities
- Use `@/lib/db` for database access
- Keep database utilities focused and single-purpose
- Use Drizzle ORM for all queries
- Export functions, not classes

### Custom Hooks
- Use `"use client"` directive in hook files
- Follow React hooks naming convention (use*)
- Keep hooks focused and reusable
- Use TypeScript for all hooks

### Utility Functions
- Keep functions pure when possible
- Use TypeScript for all utilities
- Export named exports
- Document complex logic (but avoid obvious comments)

### Example Database Utility
```typescript
import { db } from "@/lib/db";
import { user } from "@/lib/db/schema";
import { eq } from "drizzle-orm";

export async function getUserByEmail(email: string) {
  const [user] = await db()
    .select()
    .from(user)
    .where(eq(user.email, email))
    .limit(1);
  return user;
}
```

### Example Custom Hook
```typescript
"use client";

import { useState, useEffect } from "react";

export function useTemplate() {
  const [data, setData] = useState(null);
  
  useEffect(() => {
    // Hook logic
  }, []);
  
  return data;
}
```

## AI Hints
- Keep utilities focused and single-purpose
- Use `@/lib/db` for database access, not direct clients
- Follow React hooks rules for custom hooks
- Prefer pure functions when possible
- Use TypeScript strictly for all utilities
- Keep utilities testable and side-effect free when possible
